   :how "AST walk over the three scope paths. Flag every call to name/namespace/symbol/keyword/double/long/float or str/split/subs/str/starts-with? whose head argument is (a) a map lookup `(:k x)`, `(get m k)`, `(get-in m ks)` — including via :keys destructuring that later flows into those fns — when the expression is NOT wrapped in an or/some->/if-let/if-some/when-let guard and the lookup has NO default argument. Then compute the differential per file: the same key already guarded elsewhere in the same file (e.g. `(or (:reason x) :unknown)` at line 4235 but bare `(name (:reason x))` at 4212; `(double (or pressure 0))` at 4455 but `(double pressure)` at 4343) is near-certainly a bug or a missing guard — humans guard consistently within a file, so asymmetry marks the miss. Also special-case `(get-in m ks default)` and `(:k m default)`: the default does NOT protect against a nil VALUE at the path, which is what JSON-null inputs produce; flag those when the source is an HTTP/JSON parse (cheshire/clj-http in the require list). Rank: guarded-sibling-differential > json-sourced-lookup > plain unguarded lookup."
   :false-positive-risk "Code where the producer provably always supplies the key (typed records built by a constructor, e.g. infer-mode-record always setting :mode) — the lint cannot see the producer contract, so it will flag many currently-safe sites like receipt_construction's `(name (:cohort/id closed))` which is short-circuit-protected by `(keyword? ...)` in the same `and`. The guard-recognizer must understand `and`/`cond->` short-circuiting or it cries wolf on those. Mitigate by ranking, not suppressing: differential findings first, and require a human read for anything not differential."}}
